---
// Shared tool-page hero: the icon-left keyword <h1> and a value-prop subhead.
// The open-source trust chips (GitHub, MIT, Works offline) moved into
// AppBar.astro, unified with the sticky top bar the way index.astro already
// does it - see ToolPageLayout.astro, which passes them into <AppBar>.
// Keeps the single crawlable <h1> per page; the app-bar breadcrumb is nav.
interface Props {
  icon: any;
  title: string;
  subhead: string;
  slug: string;
  condensable: boolean;
}
const { icon: Icon, title, subhead, slug, condensable } = Astro.props;

// The three gradient/text-color pairs used to be three separate arbitrary-value
// Tailwind class strings (`bg-[linear-gradient(...)]` plus a `text-[var(...)]`
// each), which is 500+ bytes of escaped-selector CSS the toolPage entry sheet
// compiles once and every one of the 12 tool pages ships identically - CLAUDE.md's
// CSS duplication ratchet in styling.md charges that in full regardless of which
// single page's icon is actually redact-toned. `data-icon-tone` plus the three
// small rules below (scoped, in this file's own <style>) say the same thing in
// far fewer bytes. Sign/redact are the two tools with a tone of their own; every
// other tool leaves the attribute unset and gets the plain default below.
const iconTone = slug === 'sign' ? 'sign' : slug === 'redact' ? 'redact' : undefined;
---

<!--
  Hero geometry lives in these custom properties so the icon tile and the
  subhead/chip indent can never drift apart.
  --hero-icon      the square icon tile
  --hero-title     the <h1> font size (pinned, not inherited from the UA's 2em)
  --hero-indent    icon + gap-4, so the subhead lines up under the title
  Icon and title are flush top-aligned (`items-start`, zero margin on both) -
  deliberately not nudged to centre against the title's first line, which
  looked right for a short one-line title but left the icon visibly adrift
  from the text once a longer h1 (e.g. Compress's) wrapped to two or three
  lines and the eye compared the icon against the whole block, not just line
  one.
-->
<header class="tool-hero mx-auto w-full max-w-[1280px] px-6 pb-8 pt-14 min-[1024px]:grid min-[1024px]:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_auto] min-[1024px]:items-center min-[1024px]:gap-x-6 min-[1024px]:px-8 min-[1024px]:pb-1 min-[1024px]:pt-6 [--hero-icon:clamp(44px,8vw,52px)] [--hero-indent:calc(var(--hero-icon)_+_1rem)] [--hero-title:2rem] min-[1024px]:[--hero-icon:2.75rem] min-[1024px]:[--hero-title:2rem]" data-hero-condensable={condensable ? '' : undefined}>
  <div class="mb-3 flex items-start justify-center gap-4 min-[1024px]:col-start-1 min-[1024px]:row-start-1 min-[1024px]:mb-0 min-[1024px]:justify-start">
    <div class="tool-hero-icon flex size-[var(--hero-icon)] shrink-0 items-center justify-center rounded-2xl border-0 shadow-[0_8px_18px_-8px_var(--color-primary-ring),inset_0_1px_0_rgba(255,255,255,0.5)]" data-tone={iconTone}>
      <Icon class="h-[55%] w-[55%]" size={28} strokeWidth={1.5} />
    </div>
    <h1 class="mt-0 mb-0 text-start text-[length:var(--hero-title)] leading-[1.1]">{title}</h1>
  </div>
  <p data-hero-sub class="type-lead mx-auto max-w-[54rem] min-[1024px]:col-span-2 min-[1024px]:row-start-2 min-[1024px]:mx-0 min-[1024px]:ms-[var(--hero-indent)] min-[1024px]:mt-2 min-[1024px]:text-start">{subhead}</p>
</header>

<style>
  /* The icon tile's gradient and text color, keyed off `data-tone` (see the
     frontmatter comment above): plain CSS instead of three arbitrary-value
     Tailwind class strings, which the toolPage entry sheet used to compile in
     full and ship to all 12 tool pages regardless of which one is actually
     sign- or redact-toned. The unset-attribute case is every other tool's
     default tone, so it needs no selector of its own. */
  .tool-hero-icon {
    background-image: linear-gradient(150deg, var(--color-muted-light), var(--color-border-strong));
    color: var(--color-surface);
  }
  .tool-hero-icon[data-tone='sign'] {
    background-image: linear-gradient(150deg, var(--color-primary-hover), var(--color-primary));
  }
  .tool-hero-icon[data-tone='redact'] {
    background-image: linear-gradient(150deg, var(--color-surface), var(--color-success-soft));
    color: var(--color-success);
  }

  /* Once a file is open, the hero keeps its title line and folds the rest away.
     Reading the pitch is not what you are doing at that point - you are looking
     at your document, and on a 1512x870 laptop the pitch was costing about a
     fifth of the screen directly above it.

     `[data-tool-shell]` is the file-open signal: ToolShell renders only once a
     file is loaded, in every tool (see ToolShell.tsx). No JavaScript is involved
     and no state is duplicated - the DOM already says whether you have a file.

     This is deliberately not an SEO risk, and the reasoning matters more than
     the rule:
       - Every word stays server-rendered in the HTML, in its original order,
         with the one crawlable <h1> untouched. Nothing is moved into the island.
       - A crawler never opens a file, so `:has()` never matches and Googlebot
         renders the full hero exactly as before. It is state-driven CSS, the
         same as an open menu, not two different pages for two audiences.
       - The copy is visible by default, so it keeps full weight. Content hidden
         *by default* behind a tab or accordion is what gets discounted; this is
         the opposite.
     Do not "simplify" this into hiding the copy by default, or into removing it
     from the .astro file, which would be all three of those things at once.

     `html[data-draft-hint]` is the second, earlier gate: a returning visitor
     with a saved Sign/Redact draft gets the same collapse applied by a
     blocking script before first paint (see ToolPageLayout.astro), instead of
     waiting for `[data-tool-shell]` to appear once the async IndexedDB restore
     and hydration finish. Without it that restore is a real, input-less layout
     shift - the hero visibly collapsing a moment after the page already
     painted expanded - which Core Web Vitals' CLS metric scores. Both gates
     drive the exact same declarations so there is one visual state, not a
     hint state and a real state that could ever disagree; the hint is removed
     (by useDraftPersistence.js) if the restore turns out to have nothing to
     show, so a stale hint self-corrects instead of leaving the hero wrongly
     collapsed for good.

     Two more gates sit on top of both triggers now (E9): a `density`
     preference (`html[data-view-density='condensed']`) so the fold-away is
     opt-out instead of permanent, and a per-tool `condenseOnLoad` flag
     (`[data-hero-condensable]`, only rendered on the header when the tool
     opts in - see src/data/tools.js) so short single-card tools never fold at
     all. The draft-hint trigger already bakes the density check into whether
     `data-draft-hint` gets set (ToolPageLayout.astro's inline script only
     sets it when density is condensed), so it only needs the condensable
     gate repeated here - not the density one again.

     A third gate, viewport width, was added 2026-09-12 (Shlomi, from the
     /compress/ and /merge/ pass: picking a file used to shrink this title and
     drop the subhead at every width, which is what made the option cards and
     the primary button jump up underneath it). Below 1024px that fold-away is
     still doing real work: docs/view-density-control-spec.md 4.7 and
     src/data/tools.js record it by name for Compress and Compress Image
     specifically ("the Compress button sat ~550px below the fold" on a
     phone), and it is kept there, unchanged, for exactly that reason. At
     1024px and up the fold-away's own stated purpose - fit the page in a
     desktop viewport after a file is picked - is now met a different way: the
     dropzone/identity-row box below is a fixed 168px on desktop instead of
     the ~320px stacked dropzone that used to sit there (see
     Dropzone.module.css and ToolShell.module.css), which still buys back
     more vertical space than this fold-away used to cost. See the fit
     numbers this traded against in pick-stability.mjs's report. Confirm the
     fit again before ever reverting this split. */
  @media (max-width: 1023px) {
    :global(html[data-view-density='condensed']:has([data-tool-shell])) .tool-hero[data-hero-condensable],
    :global(html[data-draft-hint]) .tool-hero[data-hero-condensable] {
      padding-bottom: 0.75rem;

      /* Below the desktop breakpoint the hero opens with 56px (pt-14) of air
         above the title. That is the opening-statement spacing; once a file is
         open the title is orientation, and on a phone those 56px were a real
         share of what pushed a tool's primary action (Compress's button, most
         visibly) below the fold. */
      padding-top: 1.5rem;
    }

    :global(html[data-view-density='condensed']:has([data-tool-shell])) .tool-hero[data-hero-condensable] [data-hero-sub],
    :global(html[data-draft-hint]) .tool-hero[data-hero-condensable] [data-hero-sub] {
      display: none;
    }
  }

  /* Sign and Redact are the one exception to "no condensing at desktop"
     above, and the reason is a different rule than Compress's: their
     `ViewControl` (>=920px segmented Relaxed/Condensed/Full screen control,
     see docs/view-density-control-spec.md) is a user-facing preference
     whose *only* visible effect is this hero fold - there is nothing else
     for "Condensed" to do (`ViewControl.tsx`/`useViewDensity.js` only ever
     write the `data-view-density` attribute this file reads). The spec's own
     worked example is a 1512x870 laptop, i.e. desktop width; disabling the
     fold there would make that control's Condensed segment do nothing on a
     real desktop, which is not the "stop condensing on desktop" this ticket
     asked for - that request was about the automatic, permanent fold on
     tools with no control to turn it back on.

     Nothing here can reach `src/data/tools.js` or add a marker to
     `ToolShell.tsx` (both out of scope for this change), so Sign/Redact are
     told apart by `ViewControl`'s own DOM footprint instead:
     `[aria-label="View density"]`, which only that control renders and only
     once a file is open - the same moment `[data-tool-shell]` and this gate
     start mattering. Every other condensable tool (Compress included) has
     no such element and falls through to the desktop-relaxed rule above. */
  @media (min-width: 1024px) {
    :global(html[data-view-density='condensed']:has([aria-label='View density'])) .tool-hero[data-hero-condensable],
    :global(html[data-draft-hint]:has([aria-label='View density'])) .tool-hero[data-hero-condensable] {
      padding-bottom: 0.75rem;
      --hero-title: 1.75rem;
      --hero-icon: 2.5rem;
    }

    :global(html[data-view-density='condensed']:has([aria-label='View density'])) .tool-hero[data-hero-condensable] [data-hero-sub],
    :global(html[data-draft-hint]:has([aria-label='View density'])) .tool-hero[data-hero-condensable] [data-hero-sub] {
      display: none;
    }
  }
</style>
